The '''Hope and Survival Happiness Group''' ({{nihongo|希望と生存・幸福の会|Kibō to Seizon・Kōfuku no Kai}}) was a women-only [[w:new religious movement|new religious movement]] and survivalist commune founded by [[Mizue Naruse]] in [[Toyozawa]], [[Okaiken]], in 1993. The group became notorious after relocating to [[Balboa]] in 1995, where it established a fortified agricultural compound in the Warakú comarca of [[Estret Province]] that was eventually raided by the Balboan military in 1999 following the discovery of livestock theft, coercive labour practices, and illegal firearms linked to criminal networks in [[Tramuntana]].

The group's origins in Okaiken's {{nihongo|"Lost Decade"|失われた十年|Ushinawareta Jūnen}} (1993–2000) — a period of economic stagnation, corporate retrenchment, and social dislocation — made it one of the most significant examples of the era's proliferation of self-help circles, purity movements, and alternative religious communities. Its trajectory from a women's refuge to an armed enclave illustrated the broader pattern by which the Lost Decade's economic anxieties were converted into rigid communal discipline, and its eventual dissolution in Balboa exposed the legal and regulatory gaps in both nations' oversight of new religious movements.

== Name ==

The group's name — {{nihongo|希望と生存・幸福の会|Kibō to Seizon・Kōfuku no Kai}} — was a direct expression of its founding doctrine. Naruse taught that the three terms constituted a progressive path:

* '''Hope''' ({{nihongo|希望|kibō}}) — the discipline to survive; the conscious rejection of the "false comforts" of the corporate household and the lifetime employment system
* '''Survival''' ({{nihongo|生存|seizon}}) — collective living; the construction of a self-sufficient community outside the market economy
* '''Happiness''' ({{nihongo|幸福|kōfuku}}) — obedience to the group; the surrender of individual will to the communal order

The slogan was repeated in handwritten pamphlets, recorded sermons, and the banners hung in the group's compounds. It was deliberately designed to echo the language of Okaiken's postwar self-improvement culture — the same language used by corporate training programmes, [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] lay organisations, and government welfare brochures — while inverting its meaning: where the mainstream culture promised happiness through participation in the economy, Naruse promised happiness through withdrawal from it.

== History ==

=== Origins in Okaiken ===

==== The Lost Decade context ====

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group emerged from the economic and social devastation of Okaiken's {{nihongo|"Lost Decade"|失われた十年|Ushinawareta Jūnen}} (1993–2000). The collapse of the asset-price bubble in the early 1990s shattered the postwar employment model that had defined Sanese life for two generations. The [[OSE 225]] index plummeted from its December 1990 peak of 39,000 to below 15,000 by end of 1994. Land values in [[Okami]]'s commercial districts collapsed by 60% below the 1989 peak. The "zombie bank" problem — insolvent institutions kept alive through government-arranged mergers — eroded confidence in the financial system.

The political chaos of 1990–1991 compounded the economic crisis. The [[Miyazaki hotel scandal]] shattered the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]]'s grip on power, producing three prime ministers in rapid succession: [[Honda Shigehara]] (collapsed within months), [[Masaaki Fukuda]] (48 days), and [[Michio Watanabe]] (43 days — the shortest premiership in Sanese history). The [[Isao Tomita|Tomita]] government, elected in February 1993, attempted to stabilise the economy through emergency stimulus packages and a consumption tax increase (the unpopular {{nihongo|"Tea and Rice Compromise"|茶と米の妥協|Cha to Kome no Dakyō}}), but the measures had only modest short-term effect.

For women, the Lost Decade was particularly devastating. The collapse of lifetime employment disproportionately affected female workers, who had always occupied the most precarious positions in the corporate hierarchy. When companies cut long-held guarantees, women were the first to be laid off. The rise of unstable part-time employment — the {{nihongo|"freeter"|フリーター|furītā}} phenomenon — created a generation of women without job security, without pension contributions, and without the social safety net that the corporate household had once provided. Rural communities lost both population and investment, and the traditional family structures that had offered women a measure of economic protection were themselves disintegrating.

It was in this atmosphere that Mizue Naruse began holding informal study circles for women in [[Toyozawa]] in early 1993.

==== Mizue Naruse ====

{{main|Mizue Naruse}}

[[Mizue Naruse]] ({{nihongo|成瀬瑞江|Naruse Mizue}}, 1958–2019) was a former stockbroker from [[Toyozawa]] who had spent fifteen years in the securities industry before being laid off in the wave of redundancies that followed the OSE 225 crash. Her biography — a middle-class professional destroyed by the collapse of the economic system she had served — was typical of the Lost Decade's dispossessed, and it gave her a credibility that pure religious leaders lacked.

Naruse's early teachings combined survivalist rhetoric, communal labour, and a heavily moralised form of self-help. She was not a trained [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] priest, a [[Shoto]] shrine maiden, or a [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] catechist — she had no formal religious training. Instead, she synthesised elements from all three traditions into a distinctive doctrine that framed economic collapse as spiritual opportunity. The corporate world had betrayed women; the family had failed them; the government had abandoned them. The only path forward was collective self-reliance.

Naruse's personal charisma was formidable. Former members described her as {{nihongo|"the woman who saw"|見抜いた女|Minuita onna}} — a person who could identify a woman's deepest wound and make her feel that the group was the only place where that wound would be understood. She was a skilled orator who combined the cadences of [[Pure Land Baiduism|Pure Land]] devotional preaching with the language of corporate motivational seminars, producing a hybrid rhetoric that was simultaneously spiritual and pragmatic.

==== The Toyozawa commune ====

The group's first phase (1993–1995) was based in rented halls and eventually a converted warehouse in [[Toyozawa]]. During this period, the movement grew from a handful of laid-off office workers to a community of approximately 220 women. New members were recruited through word of mouth, workplace connections, and handwritten pamphlets distributed at unemployment offices and women's shelters.

The early commune operated on a simple principle: women who had been discarded by the economy would build their own economy. Members pooled their unemployment benefits, shared cooking and childcare duties, and operated a small textile repair business that provided a modest income. The daily schedule was rigorous — pre-dawn collective exercise, morning fieldwork, afternoon study sessions, evening prayer — but it was framed as a refuge from the chaos of the outside world.

The religious dimension of the group's practice was initially understated. Naruse led morning recitations of the [[Baiduism|Nian-Baidu]] — the rhythmic invocation of the Infinite Sovereign's name borrowed from the [[Pure Land Baiduism]] tradition — and evening sessions incorporated elements of [[Shoto]] purification ritual (the {{nihongo|misogi|禊}} cold-water ablution practice). However, Naruse did not present herself as a religious figure; she presented herself as a practical woman who had found a way to survive.

This changed as the group grew. By late 1994, Naruse's evening sessions had evolved from practical discussions into devotional sermons. She began to teach that the Lost Decade was not merely an economic crisis but a {{nihongo|"great crossing"|大渡り|ōwatari}} — a term borrowed directly from the [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] doctrine of the Hundred Crossings. The economic collapse was the River of Suffering; the corporate world was the River of Origin; the group was the River of the Way. This theological framework gave the group's practical activities a cosmic significance that transformed it from a self-help commune into a religious movement.

=== The legal environment in Okaiken ===

==== Article 20 and the Religious Juridical Persons Law ====

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group operated within a legal framework that was simultaneously permissive and inadequate. [[Constitution of Okaiken|Article 20]] of the [[Constitution of Okaiken]] guarantees freedom of religion and prohibits the state from compelling participation in religious acts, but it also prohibits religious organisations from exercising political authority or receiving state privileges. This framework — modelled on the postwar constitutional settlement — was designed to prevent a recurrence of the [[Honda Shogunate]]'s {{nihongo|"Sword and Sutra"|刀と経|Katana to Kyō}} system, in which [[Pure Land Baiduism]] had been integrated into the state apparatus.

The {{nihongo|'''Religious Juridical Persons Law'''|宗教法人法|Shūkyō Hōjin Hō}}, enacted in 1951 as part of the postwar legal reconstruction, provides the regulatory framework for religious organisations in Okaiken. Under the law, religious organisations may apply for status as {{nihongo|religious juridical persons|宗教法人|shūkyō hōjin}}, which grants them tax exemptions and legal personality. However, the law's certification process is deliberately minimal: the government may only verify that an organisation's purpose is religious and that its internal governance procedures are documented. It may not evaluate the validity or orthodoxy of the organisation's beliefs.

This minimal-certification standard was a direct response to the prewar state's suppression of religious dissent. The [[Honda Shogunate]] had used the {{nihongo|''terauke''|寺請}} system — the requirement that all subjects register with a state-approved Baiduist temple — as an instrument of social control, and the postwar legislators were determined to prevent any recurrence. The result was a legal framework that made it extremely difficult for the state to intervene in the affairs of religious organisations, even when those organisations exhibited signs of coercive or abusive behaviour.

==== The Naruse certification and the regulatory gap ====

Naruse applied for religious juridical person status in 1994, citing the group's Baiduist devotional practices and Shoto purification rituals as evidence of its religious character. The [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]], which administers the certification process, approved the application with minimal review — a decision that would later be criticised as emblematic of the regulatory gap that allowed the group to operate with impunity.

The certification gave the Hope and Survival Happiness Group several significant advantages:
* '''Tax exemption''' — the group's income from its textile business and member contributions was exempt from corporate taxation
* '''Legal personality''' — the group could own property, enter contracts, and receive donations in its own name
* '''Privacy protections''' — the Religious Juridical Persons Law limited the government's ability to inspect the group's internal affairs, including its financial records and membership rolls

These protections, combined with Article 20's prohibition on state interference in religious affairs, created a legal shield that made it extremely difficult for authorities to investigate allegations of abuse within the group — even as former members began to report sleep deprivation, punitive fasting, and coercive confinement.

==== The Tomita government's response ====

The [[Isao Tomita|Tomita]] government (1993–1995) was preoccupied with the economic crisis and had little bandwidth for regulatory oversight of new religious movements. The government's approach to the proliferation of cults and self-help groups during the Lost Decade was essentially passive — the Ministry of Education certified religious organisations as required by law, but it did not monitor their activities or investigate complaints unless those complaints involved criminal conduct.

This passive approach was consistent with the broader political culture. The {{nihongo|"Masugu"|真直|extra="Direct"}} cultural value — which views overt religiosity with suspicion — meant that most Sanese regarded the new religious movements of the Lost Decade as embarrassing but essentially harmless manifestations of economic anxiety. The mainstream religious institutions — the [[Pure Land Baiduism]] temples, the [[Shoto]] shrines, the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] parishes — were too established and too politically connected to be affected by the upstart movements, and they had little incentive to demand stricter regulation.

The result was a regulatory vacuum in which groups like the Hope and Survival Happiness Group could operate with minimal oversight, gradually escalating from communal self-help to coercive control without triggering any institutional response.

=== Religious doctrine and practice ===

==== Syncretic theology ====

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group's theology was a distinctive syncretism that drew on three religious traditions:

* '''[[Pure Land Baiduism]]''' — The group's devotional practice was centred on the [[Baiduism|Nian-Baidu]] recitation, the invocation of the Infinite Sovereign's name. Naruse adapted the Pure Land tradition's {{nihongo|"Easy Path"|易行道|Igyōdō}} doctrine — the teaching that individual effort is insufficient for enlightenment and that reliance on the Sovereign's grace is the only viable path — into a secularised form: individual effort was insufficient for economic survival, and reliance on the group was the only viable path. The Infinite Sovereign became a metaphor for the collective will of the community.

* '''[[Shoto]]''' — The group incorporated Shoto purification rituals, particularly the {{nihongo|''misogi''|禊}} cold-water ablution practice, which Naruse reinterpreted as a discipline of physical and mental endurance. The Shoto concept of {{nihongo|''kegare''|穢れ|impurity/pollution}} was adapted to describe the contamination of the outside world — the corporate economy, the patriarchal family, the government that had abandoned women. The group's women-only structure was justified in part through Shoto's association of ritual purity with gender separation.

* '''Survivalist feminism''' — Naruse's most distinctive contribution was the fusion of religious practice with a survivalist feminism that framed economic collapse as a gendered catastrophe. She taught that the Lost Decade was not merely an economic crisis but a {{nihongo|"male collapse"|男の崩壊|Otoko no Hōkai}} — the failure of the patriarchal economic order that had promised women protection in exchange for subordination. The group's rejection of marriage, discouragement of childbirth, and prohibition on contact with men were presented not as religious asceticism but as practical self-defence.

This syncretism was unusual in the Okaiken religious landscape, where the dominant pattern was the {{nihongo|"Two Ways"|二道|Nidō}} — Shoto for this life, Baiduism for the next. Naruse's innovation was to collapse the Two Ways into a single path: the group was both the shrine and the temple, the place of worldly survival and the vehicle of spiritual crossing.

==== The "Great Crossing" doctrine ====

By 1994, Naruse had developed the group's central theological teaching: the {{nihongo|"Great Crossing"|大渡り|Ōwatari}} doctrine. Drawing directly on the [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] framework of the Hundred Crossings, Naruse taught that the Lost Decade was the River of Suffering — the first of the Four Rivers — and that the group was building the raft that would carry women across.

However, Naruse's interpretation of the crossings diverged sharply from orthodox Baiduism. Where the Great Baidu taught that the hundred crossings required both self-effort and the grace of the Infinite Sovereign, Naruse taught that the Sovereign's grace was manifested through the group itself. The {{nihongo|"Other Power"|他力|tariki}} — the Baiduist concept of reliance on the Sovereign's Vow — was reinterpreted as reliance on the group's collective will. Naruse was the Sovereign's vessel; obedience to Naruse was obedience to the Sovereign.

This reinterpretation was heretical by the standards of both [[Pure Land Baiduism]] and the broader Baiduist establishment, but it was difficult to challenge within the group's closed environment. Members who questioned the doctrine were told that doubt was a form of {{nihongo|''kegare''|穢れ|impurity}} that contaminated the community and jeopardised the crossing. The group's internal discipline escalated accordingly.

==== Internal discipline ====

By late 1994, the group's internal regime had become markedly more coercive. Former members described:

* '''Sleep deprivation''' — Members were required to attend nightly study sessions that extended past midnight, followed by pre-dawn exercise. The resulting exhaustion made members more susceptible to Naruse's sermons and less capable of critical thought.
* '''Punitive fasting''' — Members who violated group rules were assigned {{nihongo|"purification fasts"|精進断食|Shōjin Danjiki}} of up to three days, framed as Shoto purification rituals but functioning as punishment.
* '''Contact prohibition''' — Members were required to cut all contact with family members, former colleagues, and anyone outside the group. Personal mail was intercepted and read by senior members. Telephone calls were monitored.
* '''Property surrender''' — New members were required to surrender all personal property, including savings, identification documents, and personal effects. The group controlled all financial resources.
* '''Hierarchical control''' — Naruse's inner circle (deputy [[Yoshiko Tamine]] and logistics chief [[Sayo Kishimoto]]) controlled access to food, medicine, and communication with the outside world. Dissent was punished by demotion within the hierarchy, which carried material consequences — reduced food rations, less desirable sleeping quarters, and exclusion from the group's decision-making processes.

The group's women-only structure, which had initially been a source of empowerment, became a mechanism of control. The prohibition on contact with men meant that members had no external support network to fall back on; the rejection of marriage and family meant that members had no alternative social structure to return to. The group was, in the words of one former member, {{nihongo|"both a refuge and a prison — and the door only opened from the inside."|避難所でもあり牢獄でもあり、扉は内側からしか開かなかった。|Hinansho demo ari rōgo demo ari, tobira wa uchigawa kara shika akanakatta.}}

=== The decision to leave Okaiken ===

==== Internal and external pressures ====

By early 1995, the group faced mounting pressures in Okaiken:

* '''Financial pressure''' — The group's textile business was insufficient to support 220 members, and the Okaiken tax authorities had begun to inquire about the group's financial practices despite its religious juridical person status.
* '''Regulatory scrutiny''' — The [[Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare (Okaiken)|Ministry of Health, Labour and Welfare]] had received complaints from family members of group participants alleging that their relatives were being held against their will. The ministry's investigation was hampered by the Religious Juridical Persons Law's privacy protections, but the inquiries themselves were a source of anxiety for the leadership.
* '''Media attention''' — A [[KTK]] documentary team had begun investigating the group, and Naruse feared that public exposure would undermine the group's recruitment efforts and invite further regulatory scrutiny.
* '''Space constraints''' — The Toyozawa warehouse was overcrowded, and the group's agricultural ambitions required more land than was available in urban Toyozawa.

Naruse's decision to relocate to [[Balboa]] was driven by all four factors, but the primary motivation was the search for a regulatory environment in which the group could operate with even less oversight than Okaiken provided.

==== The Balboa option ====

[[Balboa]] offered several advantages:

* '''Cheap land''' — Land in the interior of Estret Province was a fraction of the cost of comparable land in Okaiken, and the Warakú comarca's sparse population meant that the group could establish a large agricultural compound without attracting attention.
* '''Weak oversight''' — The Warakú comarca's local government was under-resourced and had limited capacity to monitor foreign religious organisations. The Balboan government's regulatory framework for religious organisations was less developed than Okaiken's, and there was no equivalent of the Religious Juridical Persons Law's certification process.
* '''Distance''' — The physical distance between Balboa and Okaiken made it more difficult for family members, journalists, and regulators to investigate the group's activities.

The group purchased a tract of land in the Warakú comarca in mid-1995 and began constructing the compound that would become known as the {{nihongo|House of Hope|希望の家|Kibō no Ie}}.

=== The Balboa period (1995–1999) ===

==== The House of Hope ====

The compound in the Warakú comarca consisted of dormitories, greenhouses, storage sheds, and a central meeting hall. It was formally women-only, though men occasionally appeared as contractors, drivers, or messengers. New arrivals were required to surrender personal property, cut contact with family members, and adopt communal schedules built around fieldwork, prayer, and instruction sessions delivered by Naruse and her inner circle.

The compound initially presented itself as a rural development project. Naruse advertised the group as a place where women could learn food production, textile work, and mutual defence against what she called the {{nihongo|"collapse of the old order"|旧秩序の崩壊|Kyūchitsujo no Hōkai}}. Local officials in Warakú initially regarded the settlement as a harmless foreign commune, especially because the group maintained orderly gardens and paid its land taxes.

==== Escalation and criminal activity ====

Over time, the compound became increasingly insular. Former members later described:

* '''Sleep deprivation, punitive fasting, and rigid internal hierarchy''' — The coercive practices that had developed in Toyozawa intensified in the isolation of the Warakú comarca, where there were no external witnesses and no regulatory oversight.
* '''"Survival requisition"''' — The group developed a habit of collecting goods from neighbouring farms under what it described as {{nihongo|"survival requisition"|生存徴発|Seizon Chōhatsu}}. Livestock disappeared from nearby corrals, crops were taken from irrigation plots, and tools were borrowed and not returned. These incidents were initially treated as rural thefts, but they later formed part of the criminal case against the cult.
* '''Black-market contacts''' — Investigators later alleged that the group financed part of its expansion through black-market contacts and through intermediaries connected to drug cartels in [[Tramuntana]]. Those links were reportedly used to obtain cash, fuel, and firearms, although the group denied acting as a cartel proxy.

The weapons matter became central to the eventual raid. Naruse framed the Balboan state as an instrument of decay and told followers that the commune must be prepared for {{nihongo|"the last hunger"|最後の飢餓|Saigo no Kiga}} — a phrase that became common in the compound's internal sermons. When Balboan police learned that the commune possessed rifles and ammunition, the case escalated from a rural property dispute into a national security issue.

==== The Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff ====

{{main|Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff}}

The Balboan military raid on the Estret compound, commonly known as the '''Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff''', was launched in 1999 after local police and agricultural inspectors compiled evidence of theft, unlawful detention, and illegal weapons possession. When troops arrived, the cult refused immediate entry and barricaded the compound, turning the operation into a tense standoff that lasted several days.

The confrontation left three people dead. Residents of nearby farms were evacuated, and roads in the Warakú comarca were closed while negotiators attempted to secure a surrender. The military later said the cult had access to firearms sufficient to threaten a prolonged siege, though the weapons were largely outdated and poorly maintained.

After a period of negotiation, the group surrendered. Several members were injured during the raid and subsequent arrests. Naruse and senior lieutenants were taken into custody, and the compound was later dismantled. Balboan prosecutors described the case as a mixture of organised theft, illegal arms possession, coercive confinement, and religious abuse.

== Aftermath ==

=== In Okaiken ===

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff became a defining reference point in Okaiken's debate over the regulation of new religious movements. The group's trajectory — from a certified religious juridical person operating under the protection of Article 20 to an armed enclave in a foreign country — exposed the inadequacy of the Religious Juridical Persons Law's minimal-certification standard and the difficulty of intervening in religious organisations that exploited constitutional protections to shield coercive practices.

The [[Banri Kaieda|Kaieda]] government (1995–1998) and the [[Katsuya Okada|Okada]] government (1998–2003) both attempted to reform the regulatory framework:

* The '''1997 Religious Organisation Transparency Amendment''' — introduced by the Kaieda government as part of the broader Financial Supervisory Agency reforms — required religious juridical persons to file annual financial disclosures and to submit to periodic audits by the [[Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology (Okaiken)|Ministry of Education, Culture, Sports, Science and Technology]]. The amendment was opposed by the major Baiduist organisations (which feared that financial disclosure would expose their own investment portfolios) and by the [[Shoto]] shrine associations (which feared that audits would compromise their traditional autonomy). It passed in a weakened form that exempted organisations with fewer than 500 members — effectively exempting the very groups most likely to engage in coercive practices.

* The '''1999 Religious Activities Review Board''' — established by the Okada government in the immediate aftermath of the Balboa standoff — created an inter-ministerial body with the authority to investigate complaints of abuse within religious organisations. However, the Board's authority was limited by Article 20's prohibition on state interference in religious affairs, and it could only refer cases to prosecutors when there was evidence of criminal conduct — not merely evidence of coercive or manipulative practices.

Both reforms were criticised as insufficient. The [[Sanese Communist Party|SCP]] argued that the Religious Juridical Persons Law needed to be fundamentally restructured to give the government the authority to decertify organisations that engaged in coercive practices, regardless of whether those practices constituted criminal conduct. The [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] opposed any strengthening of regulatory authority, arguing that it would violate Article 20 and create a precedent for state suppression of religious freedom.

The debate remains unresolved. As of 2026, the Religious Juridical Persons Law remains essentially unchanged from its 1951 form, and the regulatory gaps that the Hope and Survival Happiness Group exploited persist.

=== In Balboa ===

{{main|Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff}}

The standoff had a lasting impact on Balboan public policy. It led to:

* '''Greater scrutiny of foreign religious communes''' — The Balboan government established a registration requirement for foreign religious organisations operating in Balboa, requiring them to disclose their membership, finances, and organisational structure.
* '''Tighter oversight of land purchases by outside groups''' — The Warakú comarca's local government adopted new zoning regulations that restricted the establishment of communal agricultural compounds on land purchased by foreign entities.
* '''A more aggressive stance toward rural militias''' — The Balboan military increased its presence in the interior comarcas, conducting periodic inspections of remote compounds and ranches.

In Warakú, the compound became a warning story told by farmers and local officials whenever new groups sought isolated land for communal projects. The ruined structures of the House of Hope remained standing for several years as a physical reminder of the standoff, before being demolished in 2004.

=== Implications for religion in Okaiken ===

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group's legacy extends beyond the specific regulatory reforms it prompted. The group's trajectory illuminated several structural features of Okaiken's religious landscape:

==== The {{nihongo|"Two Ways"|二道|Nidō}} under strain ====

The traditional Okaiken pattern of religious syncretism — [[Shoto]] for this life, [[Baiduism]] for the next — depends on the stability of the social institutions that sustain it. When the corporate household, the lifetime employment system, and the extended family network are functioning, the Two Ways provide a comfortable framework for spiritual life. When those institutions collapse — as they did during the Lost Decade — the Two Ways lose their practical foundation, and the search for alternative spiritual frameworks becomes urgent.

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group was one of many new religious movements that filled the vacuum created by the collapse of the Two Ways. Its syncretic theology — combining Pure Land Baiduist devotion, Shoto purification, and survivalist feminism — was a direct response to the failure of the traditional religious institutions to address the economic and social crisis. The group's success in recruiting members demonstrated that the mainstream religious institutions had lost their capacity to provide meaningful spiritual guidance during a period of acute social dislocation.

==== The vulnerability of the {{nihongo|"Masugu"|真直}} cultural norm ====

The {{nihongo|"Masugu"|真直|extra="Direct"}} cultural value — which views overt religiosity with suspicion and encourages pragmatic, non-doctrinal religious practice — is a source of social cohesion in normal times, but it becomes a vulnerability during periods of crisis. The Masugu norm discourages individuals from seeking spiritual guidance from religious institutions, and it discourages those institutions from proactively reaching out to individuals in distress. The result is a population that is simultaneously non-religious and spiritually unmoored — a condition that makes people susceptible to the charismatic appeal of new religious movements.

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group's recruitment strategy exploited this vulnerability precisely. Naruse did not present herself as a religious figure; she presented herself as a practical woman who had found a way to survive. The group's religious practices were initially understated, emerging only after members had become emotionally and financially committed. This gradual escalation — from self-help to devotion to coercion — was facilitated by the Masugu norm's reluctance to scrutinise religious claims.

==== The institutional silence of mainstream religion ====

The mainstream religious institutions — the [[Pure Land Baiduism]] temples, the [[Shoto]] shrines, the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] parishes — were largely silent during the Lost Decade's proliferation of new religious movements. The Baiduist establishment, focused on maintaining its traditional relationship with the Imperial House and the state apparatus, did not engage with the economic anxieties of the Lost Decade's dispossessed. The Shoto shrine network, focused on seasonal festivals and lifecycle rituals, did not address the collapse of the corporate household. The Petran Catholic parishes, serving a tiny minority of Okaiken's population, lacked the institutional capacity to respond.

This institutional silence was not merely a failure of pastoral care — it was a structural feature of the Okaiken religious landscape. The Religious Juridical Persons Law's minimal-certification standard meant that the mainstream institutions had no regulatory incentive to monitor or challenge the new movements, and the Masugu cultural norm meant that the public did not expect them to. The Hope and Survival Happiness Group operated in a space that was simultaneously regulated (by the Religious Juridical Persons Law) and unregulated (by the absence of any meaningful oversight of religious organisations' internal practices).

==== The {{nihongo|"tetsukage"|鉄影}} dimension ====

The Hope and Survival Happiness Group's women-only structure and its emphasis on female autonomy placed it in the {{nihongo|''tetsukage''|鉄影|extra="iron shadow"}} — the space where people who do not fit Okaiken's behaviour-and-relationship legal framework fall because the law refuses to see them as they are. The group's members were women who had been discarded by the corporate economy, abandoned by the family system, and ignored by the religious establishment. The group offered them a space where they were seen — but the cost of being seen was submission to a coercive authority that was, in many respects, a mirror of the patriarchal order they had fled.

The {{nihongo|Marebito|稀人|extra="rare/liminal person"}} framework — which grants cultural recognition in [[Shoto]] shrine contexts for gender-diverse individuals — did not extend to the Hope and Survival Happiness Group's members. The Marebito framework operates within the Shoto shrine network, which is integrated into the mainstream religious establishment; the Hope and Survival Happiness Group operated outside that network, in the unregulated space of the new religious movement. The group's members were, in effect, twice invisible: invisible to the mainstream religious institutions that might have provided an alternative, and invisible to the legal framework that might have protected them from coercion.

